 # Rasterization Pipeline Optimization
 
+Also called: Gaussian Rasterization Optimization, Tile-Based Gaussian Rendering, Adaptive Culling for 3DGS
 
 ## Overview
-Rasterization Pipeline Optimization refers to techniques that accelerate the rendering of 3D Gaussians by improving the efficiency of the rasterization stage, addressing the bottleneck of processing non-intersecting pixels and managing memory bandwidth [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by focusing on the computational pipeline (sorting, hardware acceleration, and culling) rather than the primitive representation or shading models.
+Rasterization Pipeline Optimization refers to the family of algorithmic and hardware techniques designed to accelerate the rendering of 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) by reducing the computational overhead of tile intersection, sorting, and alpha compositing. It solves the bottleneck where standard rasterization overestimates Gaussian extents, processes non-intersecting pixels, and suffers from serial blending latency. What sets this family apart from sibling methods like [3D Gaussian Splatting Differentiable Rasterizer](2813-3d-gaussian-splatting-differentiable-rasterizer.md) (which focuses on the differentiable math) or [3D Gaussian Splatting MVP](3410-3d-gaussian-splatting-mvp.md) (which focuses on memory layout) is the explicit restructuring of the rendering pipeline stages—specifically moving culling to parallel preprocess phases, replacing coarse bounding-box tests with precise geometric checks, and co-designing hardware units for projection and sorting.
 
 ## Shared principles
-The family shares the goal of reducing the computational overhead associated with projecting and compositing 3D Gaussians onto a 2D image plane. Common mechanisms include:
-1.  **Tile-based Processing:** Dividing the image into fixed-size tiles (e.g., 16x16 pixels) to localize Gaussian-pixel interactions [S2].
-2.  **Depth Sorting:** Ordering Gaussians by depth within each tile to enable efficient front-to-back alpha compositing [S1, S2].
-3.  **Culling:** Eliminating Gaussians that do not intersect with the view frustum or specific tiles to reduce the number of projected primitives [S1, S2].
+All methods in this family operate on the standard tile-based rasterization model for 3DGS, where the image is divided into fixed-size tiles (typically $16 \times 16$ pixels) [S1, unofficial][S2]. The core optimization principle is the reduction of the "work" performed by the pixel-level render kernel. This is achieved through:
+1.  **Early Culling:** Moving the determination of Gaussian-tile intersection from the serial render stage to the parallel preprocess stage. This prevents the allocation of memory and processing time for Gaussians that do not contribute to the final image [S1, unofficial].
+2.  **Precise Intersection:** Replacing the coarse Axis-Aligned Bounding Box (AABB) test of the projected 2D covariance ellipse with precise geometric checks (e.g., ellipse-AABB intersection or adaptive radius bounding circles) to minimize false positives [S1, unofficial][S2].
+3.  **Depth Sorting:** Maintaining a depth-sorted list of Gaussians per tile to enable correct front-to-back alpha compositing, often using hardware-accelerated or radix-sort based approaches [S1, unofficial][S2].
+4.  **Alpha Compositing:** The final pixel color $C(p)$ is computed via front-to-back blending:
+    $$ C(p) = \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}} c_i \alpha_i(p) \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j(p)) $$
+    where $\mathcal{N}$ is the set of surviving Gaussians in the tile, sorted by depth [S1, unofficial]. The opacity $\alpha_i(p)$ is defined as:
+    $$ \alpha_i(p) = \sigma_i g_i(p) $$
+    where $\sigma_i$ is the Gaussian opacity and $g_i(p)$ is the projected 2D Gaussian value at pixel $p$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Speedy-Splat Rendering Pipeline](3543-speedy-splat-rendering-pipeline.md)
-A GPU-accelerated rasterization method that replaces coarse bounding-box tile intersection tests with precise Gaussian-tile intersection checks and sorting-based depth ordering. It addresses the inefficiency of standard 3D-GS rasterization, which overestimates Gaussian extents and processes non-intersecting pixels, by generating a sparse set of Gaussian-tile pairs and sorting them for efficient pixel-wise compositing. See [Speedy-Splat Rendering Pipeline](3543-speedy-splat-rendering-pipeline.md).
+A GPU-accelerated pipeline that replaces coarse bounding-box tile intersection tests with precise Gaussian-tile intersection checks and sorting-based depth ordering. It addresses the inefficiency of standard 3D-GS rasterization, which overestimates Gaussian extents and processes non-intersecting pixels, by generating a sparse set of Gaussian-tile pairs and sorting them for efficient pixel-wise compositing. Choose this method when software-based optimization is required without hardware changes, specifically to reduce the number of Gaussians processed per tile.
 
 ### [Hardware-Accelerated Gaussian Rasterization](7329-hardware-accelerated-gaussian-rasterization.md)
-A hardware-algorithm co-design that augments standard triangle rasterizers with dedicated units for Gaussian projection and blending, achieving an order of magnitude speedup in the rasterization stage. It solves the bottleneck of rendering 3D Gaussians on general-purpose GPUs by offloading specific computational patterns to specialized hardware pipelines. Unlike general-purpose compute shaders, this method isolates preprocessing and rendering into distinct hardware stages, optimizing data flow and reducing memory bandwidth requirements. See [Hardware-Accelerated Gaussian Rasterization](7329-hardware-accelerated-gaussian-rasterization.md).
+A hardware-algorithm co-design that augments standard triangle rasterizers with dedicated hardware units for Gaussian projection and blending, achieving an order-of-magnitude speedup in the rasterization stage. It solves the computational bottleneck of projecting and compositing 3D Gaussians by replacing general-purpose compute shaders with a specialized four-stage pipeline. Unlike sibling methods that rely on software-based sorting and blending on GPUs, this approach isolates near-plane culling and projection into point-based preprocessing stages, while handling sorting and rasterization in tile-based rendering stages. Choose this when hardware access or FPGA/ASIC implementation is feasible.
+
+### [AdR-Gaussian](8365-adr-gaussian.md)
+A rendering acceleration method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that optimizes the rasterization pipeline by moving serial culling operations to the parallel Preprocess stage and balancing computational loads across pixel threads. It achieves significant speedups without reducing the total number of Gaussians, thereby avoiding the quality degradation associated with pruning-based methods like EAGLES or LightGaussian. Unlike siblings that rely on hardware-specific optimizations or global Gaussian reduction, AdR-Gaussian focuses on algorithmic efficiency within the standard tile-based rasterizer by using adaptive radii and axis-aligned bounding boxes (AABB) for early culling. Choose this when maintaining high visual fidelity is critical and software-side load balancing is needed.
+
+### Splat TBO Upload & Layout
+- **Definition:** Packs per-splat data (center, covariance, DC color/alpha, optional SH) into Texture Buffer Objects (TBOs) for GPU consumption [S1, unofficial].
+- **Step:** Iterate over splats. For each splat, copy `center.xyz` as raw float bits to `d[0..2]`. Pack covariance upper-triangle to `d[3..5]` using `packHalf2x16`: `d[3]=covA[0],covA[1]`; `d[4]=covA[2],covB[0]`; `d[5]=covB[1],covB[2]`. Pack DC color/alpha to `d[6]` using `packDCColor`. Set `d[7]=0`. Create `GL_TEXTURE_BUFFER` buffer with `GL_RGBA32UI` format. Bind to `tbo_tex_`. For non-DC SH coefficients, pack each coefficient's RGB into `RGBA16F` texels (B=0), layout is `[splat0_c1, splat0_c2, …, splat1_c1, splat1_c2, …]`. Create SH TBO with `GL_RGBA16F` format [S1, unofficial].
+- **Parameter:** `sh_coeffs_per_splat_ = (active_sh_degree_ + 1)^2 - 1` (excludes DC) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Layout:** Base TBO: 2 `uvec4` texels per splat (32 B). Texel 0: `center.xyz` (float bits), `packHalf2x16(cov00, cov01)`. Texel 1: `packHalf2x16(cov02, cov11)`, `packHalf2x16(cov12, cov22)`, `packUnorm4x8(DC-baked RGBA)`, reserved. SH TBO: `GL_RGBA16F`, 8 B per non-DC SH coefficient per splat. Layout: `[splat0_c1, splat0_c2, …, splat1_c1, splat1_c2, …]` [S1, unofficial].
+- **Complexity:** Memory: 32 B/splat for base TBO + 8 B × `sh_coeffs_per_splat_` × `splat_count_` for SH TBO [S1, unofficial].
 
 ### Single-Pass Color and Depth Rasterization
-A rendering optimization where color and depth are rendered in a single rasterization process, reducing runtime compared to separate passes [S1].
+- **Definition:** A rendering optimization where color and depth are rendered in a single rasterization process, reducing runtime compared to separate passes [S2].
+- **Context:** This optimization is noted in dense RGBD SLAM methods based on 3D Gaussian representation, where rendering full-resolution images requires more time per iteration but converges faster than sparse sampling methods [S2]. It is faster than methods like SplaTAM because it avoids two separate rasterization processes [S2].
 
 ## Background
-The optimization of rasterization pipelines for Gaussian Splatting emerged as the method gained popularity for its real-time rendering capabilities. Standard rasterization approaches suffered from overestimation of Gaussian extents, leading to unnecessary computations for non-intersecting pixels [S1]. Subsequent works introduced precise intersection checks [S1] and hardware-specific optimizations to further improve performance [S2]. The method is primarily used in real-time 3D reconstruction and SLAM systems where high-fidelity rendering speed is critical [S1].
+The optimization of the 3D Gaussian Splatting rasterization pipeline emerged alongside the initial publication of 3DGS [Parent](2812-3d-gaussian-splatting.md). As 3DGS gained popularity for novel view synthesis and SLAM, the computational cost of rendering millions of Gaussians became a primary bottleneck. Early implementations used coarse bounding boxes for culling, leading to significant overdraw. Subsequent works like Speedy-Splat [S1, unofficial] and AdR-Gaussian [S1, unofficial] introduced precise geometric culling and load balancing. Concurrently, hardware-oriented approaches [S2] explored FPGA and ASIC implementations to accelerate the specific mathematical operations of Gaussian projection and sorting. The technique is widely used in real-time rendering applications, SLAM systems (e.g., SplaTAM variants), and neural rendering pipelines where frame rate is critical.
 
